<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
        Permissions for the care delegation feature, which hands a pet's checklist to whoever is
        looking after them.

        These cover its SMS channel only, where the app sends the message itself: READ_CONTACTS to
        look up the chosen contact's phone number, and SEND_SMS to deliver it. Nothing is read or
        sent until the user has picked a recipient and confirmed the exact message on a preview
        screen.

        Declaring them here is only half the job: both are "dangerous" permissions, so on API 23
        and above the app must also ask for them at runtime and keep working if the user says no.
        ChecklistFragment does that, showing a rationale dialog before each request.

        The email channel needs no permission of its own and so appears nowhere in this file. It
        sends nothing: it asks another app to open a draft, and the user addresses and sends it from
        there — which is exactly why no permission is warranted for it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SEND_SMS" />

    <!--
        Permissions for the care reminder feature, which sets an alarm for each task's time of day
        and posts a notification when it comes due.

        POST_NOTIFICATIONS became a runtime permission in Android 13 (API 33). Below that, declaring
        it here is all that is needed and notifications work at install time; from 33 the user must
        also grant it, or notify() is silently ignored. AddEditTaskFragment asks for it at the moment
        the user first gives a task a time, which is the point at which a reminder starts to make
        sense to them.

        SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM lets a reminder fire at the exact minute it is due. It is granted
        automatically on Android 12, and from Android 13 the user can revoke it, so
        ReminderScheduler checks before every use and falls back to an inexact alarm rather than
        failing. Google reserves the always-granted USE_EXACT_ALARM for clock and calendar apps, so
        it is deliberately not requested here.

        RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED is what allows BootReceiver to be started after a restart. The system
        discards every registered alarm on reboot, so without this the reminders would quietly stop
        for good the first time the user restarted their phone.
    -->
    <!--
        Lets the app see whether the device is online, which the Firestore sync uses to retry the
        moment connectivity returns — see data.sync.NetworkMonitor. Without it, a change made with
        no signal would wait for the user to edit something else or reopen the app.

        A normal permission rather than a dangerous one: it is granted at install and there is
        nothing to ask the user at runtime, because it reveals whether there is a connection, not
        anything about what travels over it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
        Lets the app open network connections: the Firestore sync, and the Open-Meteo forecast behind
        the home screen's walk-window temperature (see data.remote.weather.WeatherApi).

        A normal permission, granted at install with nothing to ask the user at runtime. It was not
        needed until now only because the Firestore SDK declares it in its own manifest and manifest
        merging folded it in; declaring it here states the app's own requirement rather than relying
        on a library's, which would vanish if that library ever did.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Permissions for the Places tab, which geotags each saved vet, groomer, park or shop.

        ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION is what capturing a place's position really needs: GPS accuracy, so the
        geotag marks the clinic's door rather than the neighbourhood. ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION is
        declared alongside it because from Android 12 the system will only show the prompt when both
        are requested, and lets the user choose "Approximate" instead — which the app accepts, with a
        note that the geotag may be off.

        Both are "dangerous" permissions, asked for at runtime and only at the moment they are used:
        pressing "Capture current location" in the place form, or "Allow location" on the Places tab
        to see distances. Each shows a rationale first, and the app keeps working if refused — see
        ui.place.LocationPermissionRequester.

        No background location is requested. The position is only ever read while one of those two
        screens is open.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
        The app is useful without a SIM — only delegation needs telephony — so the feature is
        declared as not required. Without this, Google Play would hide the app from tablets and
        other devices that cannot send SMS.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.telephony"
        android:required="false" />

    <!--
        The shake-to-reset gesture needs an accelerometer, but it is only a shortcut — every task
        can still be un-ticked by hand — so the feature is not required. Without this declaration
        Google Play would hide the app from the handful of devices that have no accelerometer.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.sensor.accelerometer"
        android:required="false" />

    <!--
        Declaring the location permissions above makes Google Play assume the app needs GPS hardware
        and hide it from devices without it. Places work without GPS — Wi-Fi and cell positioning give
        a usable fix, and every other tab needs no location at all — so both features are optional.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.location"
        android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.location.gps"
        android:required="false" />

    <!--
        android:name points at the custom Application class, which creates the Room database and
        the repositories, and starts the care reminders. Without this attribute Android would use
        the default Application and none of that wiring would exist — which would also mean the
        reminder receivers below had nothing to hand their work to.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".PetCareApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.PetCare">
        <!--
            adjustResize, so an open keyboard shrinks the layout rather than being allowed to draw
            over it — which is what lets a form's ScrollView re-measure and scroll the focused field
            into view. The alternative, adjustPan, slides the whole window up instead and would take
            the app bar off the top of the screen.

            **This declaration is necessary but not sufficient here**, and the difference is worth
            knowing: the Activity calls enableEdgeToEdge, and from Android 11 a window that does not
            fit system windows is no longer resized for the keyboard at all — the keyboard arrives as
            an inset the app has to make room for itself. MainActivity.applyWindowInsets does that.
            Removing this line would still break older devices and any future non-edge-to-edge path,
            so both halves are kept.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            Receives a care task's alarm when it comes due, shows the notification and schedules the
            next occurrence.

            Declared here rather than registered in code with registerReceiver, and that distinction
            is the whole point: a manifest-declared receiver can be started by the system when the
            app is not running, which is the normal case for a reminder. A code-registered one only
            exists while the component that registered it is alive, so every reminder would be
            missed the moment the user left the app.

            exported="false" because only this app's own alarms ever target it. Leaving a receiver
            exported would let any other app on the device fire fake reminders at the user.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".reminder.ReminderReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
            Re-registers every reminder after an event that invalidates the app's alarms.

            exported="true" is required here, unlike above: these broadcasts come from the system
            rather than from this app, and a receiver that is not exported cannot be sent one. It is
            still not open to other apps — the intent filter only matches protected system
            broadcasts, which ordinary apps are not allowed to send.

            BOOT_COMPLETED covers a restart and MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED an app update, both of which
            clear every registered alarm. TIMEZONE_CHANGED and TIME_SET cover the alarms surviving
            but now pointing at the wrong moment: a task due at 07:30 means 07:30 wherever the user
            is, so the times have to be recalculated against the new clock.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".reminder.BootReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIMEZONE_CHANGED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIME_SET" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>